From d98b9e249d9872a73500ffabf934a685ef0e717d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ctrl Kommanda <victor@ctrlbuild.tech>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 06:34:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Vercel build failure: turbo wasn't forwarding env vars to
 tasks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Real bug from the actual Vercel build log, not a repeat of the earlier
missing-generate issue (that part worked — the postinstall step
successfully generated the Prisma client). The failure was one step
later: turbo's own build pipeline (db#build, i.e. `prisma generate`
run again via the task graph) failed with the same 'Cannot resolve
environment variable: DIRECT_URL' error, even though DIRECT_URL was
correctly set on the Vercel project. Vercel's own build log named the
cause directly: Turborepo only forwards env vars into a task's
environment when they're declared in turbo.json (globalEnv or
per-task env) — anything set on the platform but missing from that
list is silently dropped from the task's process.env, strict-mode
behavior by design, not a Vercel-side issue.

Added "globalEnv": ["DATABASE_URL", "DIRECT_URL", "SESSION_SECRET",
"ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET"] to turbo.json. NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL doesn't
need listing — turbo auto-detects NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars for Next.js apps
it recognizes in the workspace.

Sandbox-verified the actual fix, not just a config-shape check: ran
`DATABASE_URL=... DIRECT_URL=... SESSION_SECRET=... turbo run build
--filter=db` directly (reproducing what Vercel's build step does) —
before this change that reproduces the exact 'Cannot resolve
environment variable' error from the log; after it, the task gets
past config loading entirely and fails only on this sandbox's
standing, unrelated network restriction to binaries.prisma.sh (the
same limitation every round has hit, and exactly where the real
Vercel build's postinstall step succeeded, confirming this is purely
a sandbox constraint, not a real problem).
---
 turbo.json | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/turbo.json b/turbo.json
index 38842c3..93a71b2 100644
--- a/turbo.json
+++ b/turbo.json
@@ -1,5 +1,11 @@
 {
   "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
+  "globalEnv": [
+    "DATABASE_URL",
+    "DIRECT_URL",
+    "SESSION_SECRET",
+    "ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET"
+  ],
   "tasks": {
     "build": {
       "dependsOn": ["^build"],
-- 
2.43.0

